  "html" : " Throughout my life, I’ve been on a journey to discover what truly matters. Like many people, I used to measure success by external standards — grades, salaries, and social status. But a series of ___(1)___ changed my perspective completely. When I was 25, I ___(2)___ a high-paying job in finance. Everyone congratulated me, but I soon realized money alone couldn’t bring happiness. Each morning, I’d wake up feeling ___(3)___ , as if something important was missing from my life. This emptiness led me to ___(4)___ volunteer work at a local shelter. There, I met people who had almost nothing yet radiated more joy than many wealthy individuals I knew. One elderly man particularly ___(5)___ me. Despite living in poverty, he spent his days teaching neighborhood children to read. When I asked why, he simply said, “This is how I ___(6)___ my life meaningful.” His words struck me deeply. I began to ___(7)___ my own values and priorities. Over time, I ___(8)___ that true fulfillment comes from contributing to something larger than yourself. I left my finance job and ___(9)___ a nonprofit organization focused on education. The work is challenging, and the pay is modest, but I’ve never felt more ___(10)___ . My definition of success has completely transformed—it’s no longer about accumulation but about ___(11)___ . This journey taught me that we all have a unique ___(12)___ to play in this world. The key is to listen to your inner ___(13)___ rather than external expectations. When you line up your actions with your core values, you’ll find the courage to pursue paths that may seem ___(14)___ to others but feel perfectly right to you. Now, when young people ask me for ___(15)___ about finding their purpose, I tell them: “Don’t rush. The answers will come when you’re ready to hear them.” ",
