  "html" : " There’s an autumn day that I recall every time I feel anxious. I was a teenager at the time, ___(1)___ in my studies and the pressure of upcoming college applications. It was my ___(2)___ , Mr. Jensen, who brought me back to earth. Mr. Jensen was a quiet man in his seventies who lived alone in the house next door. Despite our ___(3)___ difference we had a shared love for gardening, a hobby that formed an ___(4)___ friendship between us. One sunny afternoon, feeling ___(5)___ , I decided to take a break from my studies. I ___(6)___ outside to my backyard garden where I began to weed alone. Mr. Jensen was there as usual, ___(7)___ his blossoming rosebushes. Seeing my frustration, he walked over and ___(8)___ started weeding alongside me. A few minutes later, he finally spoke up. “Weeding a garden is a lot like ___(9)___ life. Sometimes, we focus so much on the weeds — the problems and difficulties — that we forget to enjoy the ___(10)___ .” He gestured to the beautiful roses in his garden, their ___(11)___ colors shining in the sun. “Don’t forget to appreciate the roses, even as you deal with the weeds.” That simple ___(12)___ hit me hard. I had been so focused on my problems that I had forgotten to enjoy my ___(13)___ — the friendships, the joy of learning new things, and the beauty of nature. That day, Mr. Jensen taught me a lesson that I’ve ___(14)___ with me ever since. Now, whenever I find myself ___(15)___ by life’s “weeds”, I pause, take a deep breath, and remember to enjoy my “roses”. ",
